{"name":"Horned Frog","hex":"#c2ae87","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"horned frog","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several large carnivorous frogs, of the genus Megophrys, Pelobatrachus, and Ceratophrys, that have elongated \"eyebrows\".","Synonym of horned lizard; genus Phrynosoma, especially the Texas horned lizard."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/horned_frog"}],"entries":[{"title":"Horned frog","blocks":[{"text":"Horned frog is a common name used to identify several kinds of frogs with hornlike features:"},{"items":["Asian horned frog, genus Megophrys of the mesobatrachian Megophryidae","Rough horned frog, genus Borneophrys of the mesobatrachian Megophryidae","South American horned frog, genus Ceratophrys of the neobatrachian Ceratophryidae","Long-nosed horned frog, genus Pelobatrachus of the mesobatrachian Megophyridae"]}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horned_frog","wikidata":"Q5904256"},{"title":"Horn (anatomy)","blocks":[{"text":"A horn is a permanent pointed projection on the head of various animals that consists of a covering of keratin and other proteins surrounding a core of live bone. Horns are distinct from antlers, which are not permanent. \nIn mammals, true horns are found mainly among the ruminant artiodactyls, in the families Antilocapridae (pronghorn) and Bovidae. Cattle horns arise from subcutaneous connective tissue and later fuse to the underlying frontal bone."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Male_impala_profile.jpg/330px-Male_impala_profile.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horn_(anatomy)","wikidata":"Q65284752"},{"title":"Frog","blocks":[{"text":"A frog is any member of a diverse and largely semiaquatic group of short-bodied, tailless amphibian vertebrates composing the order Anura. Frog species with rough skin texture due to wart-like parotoid glands tend to be 